**Theorem 6.5 (consumed whole-image memory and exclusion of atomic anchors).**
For the complete attained input of Definition 6.1, every selected set of at
least two of its four full rows has joint finite witnesses at every depth. Under
the paid complete-handoff and extra-field premises the exact minimum complete
conditional alphabet is four. No unassisted uniform finite-new-read static or
serial-forward service recovers the whole original $Q$-row. Sourcewise finite
traversal still does. No entire row stratum admits a finite residual-free
atomic-output source cover of [AAB, §2][AAB] or [PAC, Assumption 2.1][PAC].

Proof. Lemma 6.4 gives $t_{\eta,f}=4$ for each of the two synthesized finite
barriers. Theorem 4.2 gives all selected joint witnesses, and Theorems 5.2–5.4
give $M_*=M(D,Q)=4$ and the uniform-service obstruction. Equivalently, at every
depth all four finite rows meet one prefix for either fixed $f$, so four complete
field values are necessary; retaining the actual full row during paid acquisition
is a four-value zero-new-read upper bound. Cardinality four is not a preparation
or bit-cost bound. Corollary 5.5 gives the sourcewise traversal.

Every row stratum contains (FFM.31) with arbitrarily large
$\gamma(x_h)=2^{h+1}-1$. Parent mass monotonicity gives
$\gamma(U(f,x_h,z_h))\ge\gamma(x_h)$, so its entire source mass is unbounded.
A fixed residual-free source template over finite grounds, finite syntax pairs
and atomic outputs $T_{n_i+c}$ has mass bounded by the sum of the fixed ground
masses plus its number of displayed pairs: atomic masses are zero and every pair
adds at most one beyond child masses. The bound is independent of all ranks and
all correlations in their support. A finite union has the maximum of those
finite bounds. It cannot cover any of the entire row strata just exhibited.
This rules out an entire atomic-output anchor, not the existence of particular
atomic witnesses elsewhere. $\square$

